description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs:1453-1457 and 1468-1472 both use the same nested-if-let pattern:

```rust
if let Some(leaf) = &self.leaf_id {
    if leaf.matches(cert) {
        return Ok(());
    }
}
```

This is the textbook case for `Option::is_some_and` (stable since Rust 1.70 — well below the workspace MSRV of 1.73):

```rust
if self.leaf_id.as_ref().is_some_and(|leaf| leaf.matches(cert)) {
    return Ok(());
}
```

Or, given the pattern is repeated in two methods, extract a private helper:

```rust
impl<'a, R: RevocationChecker> NoCheckShim<'a, R> {
    fn bypass(&self, cert: &Certificate) -> bool {
        self.leaf_id.as_ref().is_some_and(|leaf| leaf.matches(cert))
    }
}
```

Then both call sites collapse to:

```rust
if self.bypass(cert) { return Ok(()); }
self.inner.check_revocation(cert, issuer)
```

What goes wrong if it stays: the duplicated 4-line pattern across two trait methods is bug bait — a future fix to the bypass predicate (e.g. adding a count of remaining bypasses, expanding to a list of leaves) has to be made in two places consistently. The nested-if-let form is also a clippy lint `collapsible_if` candidate; running `cargo clippy --all-features` would flag it.
